import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Demos/Lobby

meta = {
	title: "Live, channels and actors",
	description: "State that outlives a request on the Roc Solid server platform: live topics and rooms that streams share; channels, typed and checked, that pages subscribe to over one stream a tab; actors that handle one message at a time, in order, with typed asks, tells and timers, their state and timers kept in the database; and values kept in memory by key (Kv).",
	toc: [
		{ id: "rooms", label: "Rooms: who is here" },
		{ id: "topics", label: "Topics: what was said" },
		{ id: "channels", label: "Channels: live data for pages" },
		{ id: "channel-hooks", label: "What pages say, and hooks" },
		{ id: "channel-stream", label: "One stream a tab" },
		{ id: "actors", label: "Actors" },
		{ id: "asking", label: "Asking and telling" },
		{ id: "timers", label: "Timers" },
		{ id: "kv", label: "Values in memory: Kv" },
		{ id: "which", label: "Which to use" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · live, channels and actors"
				title="State that outlives a request"
			>
				A request comes and goes; some state has to stay. How many people are on a page right now, what
				someone just said to all of them: that is live state, in the server's memory, which streams share.
				A cart, a counter, a game's room: that is an actor, a piece of state that handles one message at a
				time, in the order they came, kept in the database so it is there after a restart.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="rooms">Rooms: who is here</Heading>
			<p>
				A room counts who is in it. A call joins one with <code>Live.join!</code>, and is in it until the call
				ends: a stream's, until its client goes, or it crashes. <code>Live.count!</code> says how many are in
				one, from anywhere. This stream sends the count when it starts, and again whenever someone comes or
				goes:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_online.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_online.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Live.next!(ms)</code> waits for what the call hears: <code>Heard</code> (a message, or that
				someone joined or left a room it is in), <code>Quiet</code> once <code>ms</code> have passed with
				nothing, or <code>Gone</code> when the client has gone. So a loop like this one sleeps until there is
				something to say, and ends with its client.
			</p>

			<Heading id="topics">Topics: what was said</Heading>
			<p>
				A topic carries messages to every call listening to it. <code>Live.listen!</code> starts listening (for
				as long as the call lasts), and <code>Live.publish!</code>, from any request, a job or an actor, sends
				a message to everyone listening:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_shouts.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_shouts.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A component listens to such a stream as to any endpoint's events
				(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>events</a>). That is a call on the server, paused while it waits
				(it holds no thread), and one of the six connections a browser makes to a host, for as long as it is
				open. For what pages follow live, a channel is better.
			</p>

			<Heading id="channels">Channels: live data for pages</Heading>
			<p>
				A channel is defined in a route's <code>+server.roc</code>, beside its endpoints, at a path the
				app chose: <code>channel!</code>, its check. The check is given the request, the path's params,
				and what the route's middleware handed on, and answers who the subscriber is
				(<code>Channel.allow(who)</code>), or a refusal. Its type says what is published on it, and what
				pages may say on it. This page's lobby lets everyone in:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.channel_lobby.file} source={Sources.channel_lobby.rocx} />
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo waiting="Join, here and in another tab.">
			<div data-demo="lobby"><Lobby /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> puts it in <code>Generated/Api</code> as a function of its params
				(<code>Api.channel_platform_live_lobby({"{}"})</code>; with params,
				<code>Api.channel_org_org_news({"{ org }"})</code>), as it puts endpoints, typed with the
				<code>Channel(who, message, said)</code> its <code>channel!</code>'s annotation gives. Api imports no
				route file, so a job, an actor or a store imports it and publishes by name, typed (D-037). A component subscribes
				from an effect, and the subscription ends when the effect runs again or the component goes:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.demo_lobby.file}, in part"} source={Sources.demo_lobby.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>A subscriber hears, in order, one at a time:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Resync</code></strong> each time its tab's stream (re)connects, when the server saw
					it fall behind, and when a page asks (<code>Endpoint.resync!</code>): it may have missed
					something, so it reads its data again (<code>refresh!</code>). The route's
					<code>on_open!</code> messages come right after it.
				</li>
				<li><strong><code>Message(message)</code></strong>: what was published, as the channel's type.</li>
				<li><strong><code>Refused(status, why)</code></strong>: the check refused it as the stream opened.</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Ended(why)</code></strong>: the check, run again on a beat of the stream (every 15
					s), refused it: someone who lost access stops hearing within a beat.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The server publishes with <code>Endpoint.publish!(Api.channel_org_org_news({"{ org: \"acme\" }"}), news)</code>,
				from a request, a job or an actor; only those subscribed with the same params hear it, in the
				order it was published. <code>Endpoint.publish_latest!(channel, key, message)</code> is for what
				only its latest value matters (a cursor): a subscriber behind gets only the newest with each key,
				and these aren't ordered with the channel's other messages. A subscriber that falls more than 256
				messages behind hears <code>Resync</code> instead of what it missed, never a silent gap. The page's
				own data, as the server renders it, is a <code>get!</code> on the same path, read with
				<code>Endpoint.get!</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="channel-hooks">What pages say, and hooks</Heading>
			<p>
				A route may define three hooks beside <code>channel!</code>, each given the path's params, who the
				check said the subscriber is, its tab's id, and the channel itself (to publish on):
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>receive!</code></strong>: what a page says (<code>Endpoint.say!(channel, said)</code>),
					after the same middleware and check, or a refusal. What a page says in one frame goes in one
					request.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>on_open!</code></strong>: what a subscriber is sent first, each time it subscribes
					(who is here, say), right after its <code>Resync</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>on_close!</code></strong>: told when a subscription ends, and why:
					<code>Dropped</code> (the page no longer subscribes to it, while the tab stays: the component
					went, or follows another channel now), <code>Gone</code> (the tab closed or reloaded, its network
					went, the server stopped) or <code>Refused(why)</code> (the check, at a beat). It runs outside the
					middleware: the subscriber's access may be what ended it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<code>who</code> stays on the server, kept as JSON between the check and <code>on_close!</code>. A
				tab's id is random, kept for the tab (a reload keeps it), and names the tab, not a person.
			</p>
			<p>
				A tab's hooks come in order. A channel the page drops and subscribes to again hears its end before
				its start. A tab has one stream, and when a new one comes for it (a reload, a reconnect) before the
				server has heard the old one end, the old one ends first: its subscriptions' <code>on_close!</code>
				are told before the new one's checks and <code>on_open!</code> run. So a route can keep who is here
				by tab, joining on <code>on_open!</code> and leaving on <code>on_close!</code> (at once when
				<code>Dropped</code>, after a moment when <code>Gone</code>, in case it comes back), with nothing to
				count.
			</p>

			<Heading id="channel-stream">One stream a tab</Heading>
			<p>
				A tab's subscriptions share one stream, <code>GET /rocx/live?tab=…&amp;c=/org/acme/news&amp;c=…</code>,
				whatever their number, and two components on one channel share its subscription. The runtime opens
				it once the page has rendered, and opens it again when it drops, after a wait that doubles each
				time, from half a second to 30 s. The stream says its id first; when what the page subscribes to
				changes (once the render that changed it is done), the runtime tells the stream, in one post
				naming that id, which channels it subscribes to now and which it dropped, and the stream stays
				open. With nothing left to subscribe to, it tells the stream, then closes it. A copy of the site
				that is only files answers no channels: a subscriber hears <code>Refused(404, …)</code>, asked for
				once.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>On the stream</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>event: hello</code>, <code>data: </code> the stream's id</td><td>first: what the runtime names the stream with when it changes what the tab subscribes to (192 random bits, the tab's alone)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: resync</code>, <code>data: /org/acme/news</code></td><td>in: read your data again; <code>on_open!</code>'s messages follow</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: message</code>, the URL, then <code>data: {"{\"title\":…}"}</code></td><td>what was published, as JSON</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: refused</code>, the URL, then <code>data: {"{\"status\":403,\"why\":\"Forbidden\"}"}</code></td><td>its check refused it; the stream goes on for the others</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: ended</code>, the URL, then why, as a JSON string</td><td>its check refused it at a beat</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>: beat</code></td><td>every 15 s (<code>--live-beat-ms</code>): the server is there, and checks each channel again</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>event: replaced</code></td><td>a newer stream for this tab took its place, and this one ends: a page still reading it is another tab with the same id (a duplicated tab copies it), and takes an id of its own</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>No call held.</strong> The host keeps the open streams and passes each channel's
					messages on; Roc runs only briefly, a call at a time: a check, a hook, a beat's checks. An
					endpoint's <code>events!</code> stream is a call for as long as it is open (paused while it
					waits, so it holds no thread, but it keeps its stack), and one per page rather than one per tab.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Middleware.</strong> Each check is a request the host makes of the app for the channel's
					own path, with the tab's cookies (the stream's, or the last post's that changed what it
					subscribes to: a page that signs in without a reload reaches members' channels), behind the
					app's middleware and the route's, as a <code>GET</code> of it would be. An app's sign-in guard
					guards a channel by its path, and <code>/rocx/live</code> itself never reaches the app's
					middleware: there is nothing to let through.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Gone in about 35 s.</strong> A tab whose network went never closes its stream; the next
					beat's write goes unacknowledged, and after 20 s the server drops it and tells
					<code>on_close!</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What pages say</strong> goes in <code>POST /rocx/live/send?tab=…&amp;stream=…</code>, a JSON list of
					<code>{"{\"channel\":\"/org/acme/news\",\"message\":…}"}</code>, with the stream's changes
					(<code>{"{\"channel\":…,\"subscribe\":true}"}</code>, <code>{"{\"channel\":…,\"unsubscribe\":true}"}</code>),
					answered 204, or 200 with each refusal (<code>{"{\"refused\":[{\"index\":0,\"status\":403,\"why\":…}]}"}</code>).
					One another site's page sends is refused (403), and a change naming no open stream of the tab (404).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="actors">Actors</Heading>
			<p>
				An actor is a kind and a key: the cart of member 7, the counter called <code>visits</code>. A kind
				says its name, its state when it is new (<code>initial</code>, from its key), and what it does with
				a message (<code>receive!</code>): its next state, and its reply.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_counter.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_counter.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nobody makes one, or ends one.</strong> A message to any key finds its actor as its last
					message left it, or new: <code>initial(key)</code> is where its state starts, not a message.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One message at a time, in the order they came.</strong> Each actor has a mailbox in the
					server, and handles what is in it one message after another: a hundred asks at once each see
					the state the one before left, with no lost updates and no locks to write.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A message it doesn't take is an error, not a crash.</strong> Messages are typed, so this
					is rare (a caller built against an older kind); it, a crash in <code>receive!</code>, and a kind
					the app has none of are logged as errors and told to the asker. The actor's state stays as it
					was, and it goes on with its next message.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Kept in the database</strong> (<code>rocx_actors</code>), read by its first turn and written
					after each turn that changes it. One idle for 15 minutes (<code>--actor-idle-ms</code>) is let
					go, and the next message wakes it as the database has it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> lists the kinds an app's modules define (<code>Actor.define</code>, at the
				top of a module's block, its <code>name</code> first), so the server can run an actor when nobody
				is waiting on it: a tell, a timer.
			</p>

			<Heading id="asking">Asking and telling</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ask.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ask.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Actor.ask!(kind, key, message)</code> waits for the reply, typed as the kind says, or
				<code>ActorErr</code> with why there is none. <code>Actor.tell!(kind, key, message)</code> doesn't
				wait: the message is handled after the ones before it, and its reply goes nowhere.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_tell.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_tell.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				An actor may ask another. One that asks itself, or any actor whose turn is waiting on its answer,
				is refused at once: it would wait forever. An ask not answered in 30 s is given up on, and its
				message is dropped if its turn hasn't come.
			</p>

			<Heading id="timers">Timers</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>receive!</code> is given the actor itself (<code>me</code>): its key
				(<code>Actor.key_of(me)</code>), and its own timers. <code>Actor.after!(me, ms, message)</code>
				sends it a message later, typed as its kind's messages are, and answers the timer, which
				<code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops. A timer every so often is one that sets the next:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ticker.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ticker.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Actor.tell_after!(kind, key, ms, message)</code> does the same from anywhere: a request, a
				job, another actor.
			</p>
			<p>
				With a database, a timer outlives a restart: it is a row (<code>rocx_actor_timers</code>), written
				at once (outside any transaction the caller has open, as a tell is sent at once) and deleted when
				the turn that handled it has ended, and the server sets every row again as it starts. Its message
				is told at least once: a server killed in the middle of that turn tells it again after its restart.
				<code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops a timer that is waiting, or one that has fired whose turn hasn't
				come. Without a database, timers are in memory, and a restart loses them.
			</p>

			<Heading id="kv">Values in memory: Kv</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Kv</code> keeps values by key in the server's memory, for as long as you say or until it stops:
				never written anywhere, so a restart forgets them. For what is cheap to lose and read on every
				request: a rate limit's counts, a cache, a one-time token. A value is any type JSON can write, read
				back as the type its reader asks for.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_kv_limit.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_kv_limit.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_kv_once.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_kv_once.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>{"Kv.set!(key, value, { ttl_ms })"}</code> and <code>Kv.get!(key)</code>; <code>Kv.delete!(key)</code>.
					A time to live of 0, the default, keeps it until the server stops.
				</li>
				<li>
					Each operation is atomic. <code>Kv.increment!</code> counts every request (a new counter's time to
					live starts with it, so it counts in windows); <code>Kv.add!</code> lets one request in (a claim, an
					idempotency key); <code>Kv.take!</code> gives a value once.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"Kv.push!(key, value, { keep })"}</code> and <code>Kv.list!(key)</code>: a capped list, newest first.
				</li>
				<li>
					It holds 64 MiB (<code>--kv-max-mb</code>, or <code>KV_MAX_MB</code>); past that the least recently used values go. Keys name
					their owner first (<code>login:…</code>, <code>email:mailbox</code>), so a package's never meet an app's.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="which">Which to use</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>For</th><th>Use</th><th>Lasts</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>who is here, now</td><td>a room (<code>Live.join!</code>, <code>Live.count!</code>)</td><td>while the calls in it last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>telling everyone listening</td><td>a topic (<code>Live.publish!</code>, <code>Live.listen!</code>)</td><td>while the listeners last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>live data for pages, checked</td><td>a channel (<code>channel!</code>, <code>Endpoint.subscribe!</code>, <code>Endpoint.publish!</code>)</td><td>while the subscribers last</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a count, a cache, a token used once</td><td>a value in memory (<code>Kv.increment!</code>, <code>Kv.set!</code>, <code>Kv.take!</code>)</td><td>its time to live, or until a restart</td></tr>
						<tr><td>state many requests change, one at a time</td><td>an actor (<code>Actor.ask!</code>, <code>Actor.tell!</code>)</td><td>in the database: restarts too</td></tr>
						<tr><td>data, queried every way</td><td>a table (<a href={Paths.platform_database}><code>Db</code></a>)</td><td>in the database</td></tr>
						<tr><td>work that may fail and should run again</td><td>a job (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}><code>Job.enqueue!</code></a>)</td><td>in the database, until done</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>An actor's state is JSON</strong> in one row, written after each message that changes it: fine for a cart, not for a ledger.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Tells are in memory.</strong> A restart loses those not yet handled: a job is for work
					that must survive one. Timers outlive it, with a database.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests open streams that join a room and count each other, publish to listeners,
					open one stream for many channels (one refused), say things on one, end one whose check fails at a
					beat, fall behind on one, change what one carries by its id (a channel dropped is told
					<code>Dropped</code>, the stream's end <code>Gone</code>, in order; a post from another site or with
					another tab's id refused), and keep 40 streams open on two Roc threads while requests still answer; in a
					browser, two components share one stream, a change is told the stream without a reconnect, and a
					drop reconnects a little later each time;
					ask an actor a hundred times at once, crash one, have one ask another, itself and one waiting on
					it, tell one in order, set and cancel timers, send one a message it doesn't take, let one go
					when idle and wake it, and restart the server to find an actor's state where it was and its timer
					told once after the start; and in
					<code>Kv</code>, let a value expire, count 200 increments at once, cap a list, and go past the limit
					to see the least recently used values go.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
